// batch ad: dogali, yalu1894, coolela, adwa, canudos, omdurman
Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "ras": "Ethiopian title for a great lord, given to the governors of the empire's major regions. Each ras commanded his own army.",
  "askaris": "African soldiers recruited, paid and trained by the European colonial armies. In Eritrea, the Italians formed entire battalions of askaris.",
  "bersaglieri": "Elite infantry of the Italian army, famous for their feathered hats and for marching at a running pace.",
  "alpini": "Mountain troops of the Italian army, created in 1872 to defend the Alps.",
  "tratado de wuchale": "Treaty signed in 1889 between Italy and Menelik. The Italian and Amharic texts said different things in Article 17, and that difference became the pretext for the war.",
  "régulo": "A local African chief, subordinate to a king or to the colonial government, in the Portuguese vocabulary of the time.",
  "jagunço": "An armed man of the northeastern backlands, in the service of a boss. Soldiers came to use the word for the followers of Antônio Conselheiro.",
  "arraial": "A small settlement in the Brazilian interior. Canudos was called an arraial by outsiders and Belo Monte by its residents.",
  "caatinga": "The dry, thorny vegetation of the northeastern backlands. It hides those who know the ground and punishes those who do not.",
  "gravata vermelha": "Name given, in the Canudos war, to the throat-cutting of prisoners.",
  "zariba": "An improvised fence of thornbushes and branches, used in Sudan to protect a camp.",
  "dervixe": "Name the Europeans gave to the Mahdist soldiers. They called themselves the ansar, the partisans of the Mahdi.",
  "maxim": "An automatic machine gun invented by Hiram Maxim. A single gunner fired hundreds of rounds a minute, and that changed colonial wars.",
  "khor": "A dry riverbed or gully in the Sudanese desert. It only fills in the rainy season and can hide an entire army.",
  "sirdar": "Title of the commander in chief of the Egyptian army. Kitchener was the sirdar.",
  "torpedeira": "A small, fast ship that carried torpedoes, weapons launched against the hulls of enemy ships.",
  "canhão de tiro rápido": "A medium-caliber gun with a mechanism that reloaded quickly. A ship with many of them poured out more shells per minute than one with a few large, slow guns.",
  "linha de fila": "Ships in a column, one behind the other, so that all can use their side guns against the enemy."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  dogali: {
    war: "Italian expansion on the Red Sea and Ethiopian resistance (1885–1896)",
    hook: "About 500 Italian soldiers climbed a hill and did not come down: the first warning that Ethiopia would not be easy to conquer.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Italy only became a country in 1861 and came late to the race for colonies. In 1882 it took the bay of Assab, on the Red Sea, and in February 1885 it occupied the port of Massawa without firing a shot, with the approval of the British government. From there, the next step was to climb to the highlands, where Ethiopia lay.",
        "Ethiopia was one of the few African kingdoms that had never been colonized. Its emperor, Yohannes IV (Giovanni IV, to the Italians), was a Christian, had a large army and understood the highland lands to be his. The man who guarded that frontier was [[Ras|ras]] Alula Engida, governor of the region, based in Asmara.",
        { h: "The road to Dogali" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1882", "Italy takes the bay of Assab."],
          ["Feb. 1885", "Occupies Massawa without a fight."],
          ["Soon after", "Takes Wia, Zula and the village of Saati, and builds a small redoubt there on the heights overlooking the caravans' water."],
          ["25 Jan. 1887", "Ras Alula attacks the Italian fort at Saati and is repelled."],
          ["26 Jan. 1887", "An Italian column carrying supplies to Saati is surrounded and destroyed at Dogali."]
        ]},
        "Saati lay in an area that, on paper, belonged to Egypt, but which Ethiopia had controlled for a long time. Alula warned that further Italian advances would be treated as hostile. The Italians answered by reinforcing the fort. War was one step away."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Ras Alula Engida" },
        "Governor appointed by Yohannes IV over part of the highlands of present-day Eritrea, based in Asmara. His job was to guard that frontier, and he warned that the Italian advance violated a treaty between Ethiopia, Egypt and Great Britain. On 25 January he attacked Saati on his own initiative and lost hundreds of men to the fort's cannon and rifles, against only four Italian wounded, according to English Wikipedia. He fell back. The next day, instead of repeating the assault, he decided to attack whoever came to relieve the fort.",
        { h: "Lieutenant Colonel Tommaso De Cristoforis" },
        "He commanded the relief column. Major Giovanni Battista Boretti, who was defending Saati, had asked the neighboring fort of Moncullo for food and ammunition, and it was De Cristoforis who set out with them. He was later awarded, posthumously, the Gold Medal of Military Valor, Italy's highest decoration."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Italian column had 548 men, according to Italian Wikipedia: companies of infantry regiments 15, 16 and 41, detachments of regiments 6 and 7, and a section of two machine guns commanded by Captain Carlo Michelini di San Martino. English Wikipedia speaks of about 500 Italians and 50 Eritrean irregulars.",
        "The Ethiopians are harder to count. English Wikipedia gives 10,000 to 15,000 warriors, citing Clodfelter. Italian Wikipedia speaks of about 25,000 men in the attack on Saati, the day before. By either count, it was thousands against a few hundred.",
        { note: "The Ethiopian numbers are not firm. We use the range of 10,000 to 15,000 for the battle of Dogali itself; the 25,000 refers to the attack on Saati, the day before, according to Italian Wikipedia." },
        { h: "The battle" },
        "The column set out to take supplies to the besieged fort. Alula chose to attack it, instead of returning to the assault on the fort. The Italians were ambushed and fell back to a small hill that overlooked the valley. There they grouped together and fired until the ammunition ran out.",
        "The machine guns did not help as they should have: English Wikipedia says the Gatlings jammed. When the ammunition ran out, the fight turned hand to hand, against spears and swords. According to the Italian account, the resistance lasted about four hours, and the commander, De Cristoforis, was killed by spear blows. The column's doctor, Dr. Salimbeni, was captured and forced to treat wounded Ethiopians. A second Italian column arrived after the end and found only a few wounded.",
        { note: "There is no reliable step-by-step description of the column's route or of the exact shape of the valley. That is why this case has no diagram." }
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Italian losses are generally given at around 430 dead, including 23 officers, and about 80 wounded. Other authors reach higher numbers: 500, 730 and even more than a thousand, depending on the source. Ethiopian losses, by Italian estimates, came to nearly a thousand dead.",
        "Italy abandoned Saati and a few nearby posts, but did not give up. Francesco Crispi's government sent about 18,000 soldiers with modern weapons. Yohannes IV marched against them in 1888 and ended up withdrawing, for lack of supplies and because of Mahdist attacks on the western border. In 1890, Italy officially founded the colony of Eritrea.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Dogali was a small defeat and an enormous scandal in Italy. It moved the country and fed a desire for revenge. In practice, it pushed the government toward a bigger gamble: backing Menelik, Yohannes's rival inside Ethiopia itself. The end of that line is Adwa, nine years later.",
        "Rome still keeps the memory of the battle: the great square in front of Termini station is called Piazza dei Cinquecento, in honor of the fallen soldiers, and near it stands an ancient Egyptian obelisk, put up as a monument.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The number of dead: 'the five hundred' in the square's name is an approximation. The sources give from 430 to more than a thousand." },
        { note: "Ethiopian memory: in 1987, on the centenary, Ethiopia's military government (the Derg) put up a monument to Alula and the victory at Dogali. To many Eritreans, Alula was a ruler from outside, not a hero. The monument is said to have been destroyed by Eritrean forces around 1989, and by 1996 it no longer existed." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  coolela: {
    war: "Portuguese campaigns against the Gaza Empire (1895)",
    hook: "In about forty minutes, a square of rifles and cannon ended the power of the last great king of southern Mozambique.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "The Gaza Empire arose in southern Mozambique in the 19th century, founded by Soshangane, a chief who fled the Zulu world. His successors ruled as lords over a people who were not native to the place: the Vatua, speakers of Nguni, commanded Ndau, Tonga, Chopi and other peoples, who paid tribute and supplied warriors.",
        "The man on the throne in 1895 was Gungunhana, called the 'Lion of Gaza' by the Portuguese. Portugal had trading posts and a port in the region, Lourenço Marques, but little real power. The European treaties of 1891 left Gaza inside the Portuguese sphere, and an agreement that Gungunhana made in 1890 with the British South Africa Company worried Lisbon. The royal commissioner António Enes and the military wanted to annex the territory, and not merely accept a protectorate.",
        { h: "The road to Coolela" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1890–1891", "Gungunhana grants concessions to the British company; the European treaties put Gaza in the Portuguese sphere."],
          ["1894–1895", "Attacks on Lourenço Marques by chiefs tied to Gaza. Portugal demands the handing over of two [[chiefs|régulo]] and an annual payment of 10,000 pounds in gold, as well as the right to collect taxes."],
          ["3 June 1895", "The northern column, with about 1,200 men, lands at Inhambane."],
          ["7 Nov. 1895", "The Gaza army attacks the Portuguese camp in the Coolela valley."]
        ]},
        "Gungunhana probably wanted to avoid the confrontation, but, according to English Wikipedia, the court faction that favored war prevailed, and the army was assembled."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Gungunhana" },
        "Emperor of Gaza, king of a large kingdom in which many peoples were vassals and paid tribute. The sources differ on who commanded the army at Coolela: some give the command to Maguiguana, leader of the court's war faction, others to a chief called Mahougné. None of those we consulted shows Gungunhana on the battlefield.",
        { h: "Colonel Eduardo Galhardo" },
        "Commander of the Portuguese column. He went up the valley with sick and tired troops, and at the decisive moment was seen riding along the ranks to restore fire discipline. After the battle, he is said to have compared the fight to a 'cotillion', a kind of ballroom dance.",
        { h: "Mouzinho de Albuquerque" },
        "A cavalry officer who stood out in the campaign. At Coolela he was not the commander, but in December he took the most famous deed: the capture of Gungunhana."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Galhardo's column landed with about 1,200 men. Disease and poor logistics took a heavy toll: by the time the column advanced, the Europeans had been halved, and of the 2,700 African auxiliaries about a fifth were left, according to English Wikipedia. That source's infobox gives, on the battlefield, 631 Europeans, 100 sepoys and 500 African auxiliaries, with 6 guns.",
        "The Gaza army is given as between 8,000 and 20,000 men, with 2,000 to 3,000 rifles, depending on the source. There was also an internal weakness: about five in every eight were Chopi, Tonga and Ndau vassals, with no wish to die for Gungunhana, and the most experienced general is said to have refused to take part. Gungunhana had gathered, at most, a third of the warriors he could have.",
        { note: "The numbers for Coolela vary widely from source to source, for both Portugal and Gaza. We use the ranges and make clear where they differ." },
        { h: "The terrain and the night before" },
        "After three days of marching, the column camped on the shore of the Coolela lagoon, in the valley of the same name.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        "In the first hours of 7 November, the Gaza army appeared no more than 250 meters from the Portuguese square, advancing fast. The Portuguese formed the [[square|quadrado]], with rifles facing outward on every side. The fire began disorganized and grew steadier as Galhardo walked the ranks.",
        "The Gaza center attacked the front of the square. When the Portuguese stopped firing to let the smoke clear, two groups of warriors nearer in, the 'Buffaloes' and the 'Crocodiles', thought the enemy was wavering and attacked the left flank with all their force. It was a suicidal charge. About forty minutes after the first shot, the Gaza warriors fell back, pursued by Portugal's African auxiliaries. There are reports of machine guns being used."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Portuguese losses were small: about 5 dead and between 21 and 33 wounded, depending on the source. Gaza's are estimated at from a few hundred to 1,500, dead and wounded together, and the sources do not agree. The army scattered, and Gungunhana fled.",
        "Four days later, Galhardo marched on Manjacaze, the capital, and found it abandoned and burned. On 14 November, back at Chicomo, the column received the submission of several chiefs of the region.",
        "In December, Mouzinho de Albuquerque, now military governor of Gaza, set out with a small force, some 47 Europeans and 200 auxiliaries, and reached Chaimite, the old capital, where Gungunhana was. It was around 28 December (some sources say 27), early in the morning and without resistance. Gungunhana was tied up and deposed. Taken to Lourenço Marques and then to Lisbon, where he arrived on 13 March 1896, he was exiled to the Azores and died there in 1906.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Coolela shows the pattern of colonial wars of the era: a large and brave army, but armed with spears and old rifles, against a few soldiers with rapid-fire weapons and a defensive formation. It opened the way to the end of Gaza's independence. Maguiguana still led a revolt to restore Vatua power, but was defeated and killed at Macontene, in July 1897, which closed the question of Portuguese sovereignty.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The Gaza command: the sources give different names (Maguiguana, Mahougné), and one points to Gungunhana himself. On the Portuguese side, Galhardo's command is the consensus, although one of the texts consulted cites Mouzinho." },
        { note: "The date of Gungunhana's capture, 27 or 28 December, changes from source to source. Most of those I consulted give 28." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  adwa: {
    war: "First Italo-Ethiopian War (1895–1896)",
    hook: "In a single day in March 1896, an African army destroyed a modern European force and secured its country's independence.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "By 1896, almost all of Africa already had an owner. Britain, France, Germany, Belgium, Portugal and Italy had divided up the continent around a table, at European conferences, and Ethiopia was one of the few exceptions. Italy, a young and insecure country, wanted its place in the club: it already had Eritrea, on the Red Sea coast, and wanted the highlands.",
        "The pretext came from a treaty. In 1889, Italy signed with Menelik, king of Shewa, the [[Treaty of Wuchale|tratado de wuchale]]. Article 17 said, in the Italian text, that Menelik would use Italy to deal with other countries, which would make Ethiopia a [[protectorate|protetorado]]. In the Amharic text, it said only that he could use Italy's 'good offices' if he wished. The Italians said Menelik knew what he was signing. Menelik rejected the treaty in 1893.",
        { h: "The road to Adwa" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1889", "Treaty of Wuchale. Article 17 has two meanings."],
          ["Jan. 1895", "General Oreste Baratieri defeats Ras Mengesha of Tigray at Coatit."],
          ["Sept. 1895", "Menelik summons the army of the whole empire, the awaj."],
          ["7 Dec. 1895", "The Italians are defeated at Amba Alagi."],
          ["Jan. 1896", "The Italian garrison of Mekelle, without water, surrenders and is allowed to leave freely."],
          ["29 Feb. 1896", "Italian council of war: Baratieri decides to attack."],
          ["1 Mar. 1896", "Battle of Adwa."]
        ]},
        "By the end of February 1896, both sides were going hungry. Baratieri thought Menelik's army would fall apart when the local food ran out. But Rome, the capital, demanded a victory, and the general was about to be replaced."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Menelik II, 51 years old" },
        "Emperor of Ethiopia since 1889, he came from Shewa, in the south, and had bought modern rifles and cannon. By the time of Adwa, he had gathered the armies of nearly every region of the country, from Shewa, Harar, Gojjam, Tigray, Wollo, Gondar and others. When the fighting began, he was praying in the church of St. George, beside the abuna Mattheos.",
        { h: "Taytu Betul" },
        "Empress and wife of Menelik, she commanded her own soldiers, from Semien. At Mekelle, the strategy of cutting off the fort's water is attributed to her by at least one source. At Adwa, according to the English Wikipedia narrative, it was she, together with Ras Mengesha, who convinced Menelik not to withdraw and to throw his reserve into the battle.",
        { h: "Oreste Baratieri" },
        "Governor of Eritrea, an experienced general. He had won at Coatit, but now he was in a dilemma: if he withdrew, he would lose Rome's support; if he attacked, he exposed an army without good maps. At the council of 29 February he said the provisions would last less than five days. His brigadiers, among them Albertone, Arimondi, Dabormida and Ellena, argued that withdrawing would sink morale. Baratieri hesitated and, in the end, ordered the attack.",
        { h: "Other names" },
        "On the Ethiopian side, Ras Makonnen (Harar), Ras Mengesha and Ras Alula (Tigray), Ras Mikael (Wollo, with the Oromo cavalry), Ras Tekle Haymanot (Gojjam) and fitawrari Gebeyehu. On the Italian side, General Matteo Albertone, who commanded the [[askari|askaris]] battalions, and Vittorio Dabormida, Giuseppe Arimondi and Giuseppe Ellena."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Estimates of the Ethiopians range from 73,000 to more than 100,000 men. The count that Wikipedia makes by region gives about 80,000 rifles, plus some 20,000 with spear and sword, and 42 guns. The camp followers were nearly as many as the fighters. On the Italian side, the sources differ: from 14,500 combatants (Marcus) to 17,770 (Lewis), of whom 10,596 were Italians and 7,104 askaris, with 56 guns.",
        "The Italians were divided into four brigades. Albertone commanded the native battalions, with 4,339 rifles and 14 guns. Arimondi, the 1st brigade, with [[bersaglieri]], 3,324 rifles and 12 guns. Dabormida, the 2nd, with 4,833 rifles and 18 guns. Ellena, the 3rd, the reserve, which included [[alpini]], with 3,032 rifles and 12 guns.",
        { note: "The numbers for Adwa are the most debated of the whole war. Here we follow the ranges that modern historians accept, not those of a single author." },
        { h: "The night march" },
        "On the night of 29 February, the three front brigades set out in separate columns, with Ellena's reserve behind, to take three mountain ridges. Albertone, on the left, was to reach Kidane Mehret and set the pace, Arimondi would stay in the center and Dabormida on the right. The map had been made from local accounts and was wrong. The moon helped, but nobody knew exactly where they were.",
        { h: "The battle in six movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, Albertone got lost. At around two thirty in the morning, he veered north, and the columns stopped moving together. Following local guides, he reached the Kidane Mehret pass and went beyond what was ordered, as far as Mount Abba Gerima, where he was cut off from the rest of the army. An Ethiopian informer who worked as Baratieri's interpreter warned Ras Alula, and the Ethiopian chiefs took up their positions.",
        "Second, at around six in the morning, Albertone's vanguard was hit by an Ethiopian charge coming from the mountains of Enda Garima and Gessoso. The Italian mountain batteries were taken, and the two commanders died. Albertone threw in his reserve and pushed the Ethiopians back about a kilometer. His askaris held out for more than two hours, and the artillery caused many casualties, until fitawrari Gebeyehu died at the front, hit by a machine gun.",
        "Third, Dabormida took the wrong road. He went into a valley and drew away from the rest of the army, opening a gap of about three kilometers. Ras Makonnen slipped into that gap and isolated him. Baratieri's messages, trying to reorganize everything, did not reach the brigadiers.",
        "Fourth, and decisive: Menelik, who had arrived at around eight, hesitated and thought of withdrawing. Taytu and Mengesha convinced him to launch the reserve, of about 25,000 men. The guard attacked head-on, Taytu's troops on the right flank and Mikael's on the left. At around eight thirty, Albertone's brigade, surrounded, began to fall back, and the retreat turned into flight. Albertone was captured when his mule was killed.",
        "Fifth, the center. From nine to noon, Ethiopian columns fell on Arimondi. A column of 25,000 men from Shewa took a rocky spur that wrapped around the Italian right flank. Galliano's battalion collapsed: of its 23 officers, ten died. Of the 24 companies of Ellena's reserve, only five were free to help, and the 5th infantry regiment and the alpini, sent to plug the left flank, were crushed. At around noon, the center gave way, and Baratieri ordered a general retreat. Mikael's Oromo cavalry fell on those fleeing. About a hundred alpini under Captain Cella held out to the end and gave the others time.",
        "Sixth, Dabormida's brigade. Isolated, it spent a few hours in a false calm. At around one in the afternoon, it sent Baratieri a message saying the situation was good, and the message never arrived. Dabormida realized he was on the wrong road, but chose to attack rather than withdraw. The final charge, to the cry of 'Avanti, Savoia!', was repelled. The retreat through the valley was blocked. Lieutenant Rayneri held a pass and saved many survivors. Dabormida vanished at the end of the fight, and the exact manner of his death is uncertain."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Italian and colonial losses, in dead, came to around 6,000: 5,900 (Caulk) to 6,133 (Berkeley), about 2,000 of them askaris. Prisoners range from at least 2,000 to about 3,900, Italians and askaris together. Italy lost all its artillery, about 11,000 rifles and most of its transport. The Ethiopians had from 4,000 to 7,000 dead and from 6,000 to 10,000 wounded.",
        "Albertone was treated comfortably in Taytu's camp. The captured askaris, seen as traitors, had their right hand and left foot cut off, about 800 of them, according to accounts. About 70 Italian prisoners were massacred in reprisal for the death of an Ethiopian chief, and the responsible officer is said to have been jailed by Menelik.",
        "Menelik did not pursue the Italians into Eritrea. The reasons discussed: food was short, the army was exhausted, the rainy season was approaching, horses were few and he feared that total annihilation would push Italy into a national war. Ras Alula wanted to pursue. Others think it was a lack of boldness.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Italy had street demonstrations in several cities, and Francesco Crispi resigned on 9 March. In October 1896, the Treaty of Addis Ababa recognized Ethiopia as an independent state. The historian Paul Henze called Adwa a fundamental turning point in Ethiopian history. It is one of the greatest European reverses of the partition of Africa, and became a symbol of independence for African nationalists and pan-Africanists. Independence lasted until 1935, when Fascist Italy invaded: on taking Addis Ababa, in 1936, Mussolini proclaimed 'Adua è vendicata'.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Article 17 of the treaty: the Italian text and the Amharic one said different things. Italian diplomats maintained that Menelik knew this. That mismatch is the formal origin of the war." },
        { note: "The time of departure: English Wikipedia dates the brigades' departure 'around 9 p.m. on the 27th', which does not fit with the council of war on 29 February. We follow the order of events: the march was on the night of 29 February to 1 March." },
        { note: "The weapons: the newest rifles, the Carcano model 1891, were not handed out, because Baratieri wanted to use up the ammunition for the old rifles first." },
        { note: "Effect on Ethiopia: the historian Donald Levine argues that the victory strengthened isolationist and conservative forces, which resisted importing Western techniques." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  canudos: {
    war: "War of Canudos (1896–1897)",
    hook: "A town of poor people, without an army, defeated three military expeditions of the Republic before being destroyed by the fourth.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Brazil in 1890 was a country of rapid change and much insecurity. Slavery had ended in 1888, the monarchy fell in 1889, and the young Republic faced the Revolta da Armada and the Federalist Revolution, which only ended in 1894 and 1895. In the backlands of Bahia, a terrible drought afflicted the population between 1888 and 1892.",
        "It was in this world that a wandering preacher, Antônio Maciel, born in Ceará in 1830, gained thousands of followers. He was known as Antônio Conselheiro. He wandered the backlands for about 25 years, counselling, praying and building churches and reservoirs. To him, the Republic was the work of the Devil. In 1893, his followers settled on an old ranch, Canudos, which they renamed Belo Monte.",
        { h: "The road to war" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1893", "Conselheiro and his followers settle in Canudos."],
          ["21 Nov. 1896", "First expedition, at Uauá: about 100 soldiers are driven back."],
          ["Jan. 1897", "Second expedition, under Major Febrônio de Brito, withdraws."],
          ["Mar. 1897", "Third expedition, under Colonel Moreira César, is destroyed."],
          ["Jun. 1897", "Fourth expedition, under General Artur Oscar."],
          ["22 Sept. 1897", "Antônio Conselheiro dies."],
          ["5 Oct. 1897", "The last stronghold falls."]
        ]},
        "The trigger, according to English Wikipedia, was a quarrel over timber ordered for a new church, which a local judge blocked. The underlying cause was wider: ranchers missed the labor that was migrating to the settlement, and a rumor circulated in the press that the settlement was a monarchist conspiracy to restore the Empire. Later research shows that Conselheiro stayed within Catholic orthodoxy and had good relations with the local clergy."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Antônio Conselheiro" },
        "A religious leader, not a military one. The organization of Belo Monte had war commanders, among them João Abade, Pajeú and Joaquim Macambira. Recent research sees, in the decision to go to Canudos, a rational choice by people who were looking for safety. Conselheiro died on 22 September 1897, probably of dysentery, or because he refused food after the sacred places were destroyed.",
        { h: "Moreira César" },
        "An Army colonel, he had helped put down earlier revolts. He commanded the third expedition, with about 1,300 men, 4 Krupp cannon and 15 million cartridges. He was shot during the attack and died in the early hours of the next morning.",
        { h: "Artur Oscar de Andrade Guimarães" },
        "General who commanded the fourth expedition. Marshal Carlos Machado de Bittencourt, the Minister of War, went to Monte Santo to take care of the troops' supply.",
        { h: "Euclides da Cunha" },
        "Journalist and engineer. He covered the war for a São Paulo newspaper, and from 16 September to 3 October 1897 was near the front, and left ill two days before the end. He wrote 'Os Sertões' (1902), the narrative that shaped the image of Canudos for decades."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The first and second expeditions" },
        "The first expedition, of about 100 soldiers under Lieutenant Manuel da Silva Pires Ferreira, faced, on 21 November 1896, roughly a thousand backlanders at Uauá. It lost 10 dead and 16 wounded, and counted 150 dead on their side. It withdrew to Juazeiro.",
        "The second, under Major Febrônio de Brito, set out with 543 soldiers, 2 Krupp cannon and 4 Nordenfelt machine guns. It waited about 15 days at Monte Santo, which gave the backlanders time to prepare, burning ranches within a radius of about three leagues to empty the countryside. On 18 January 1897, after a skirmish of about three hours at Cambaio, the soldiers came close to the settlement, but went hungry. On the 19th, at dawn, they were attacked en masse and decided to withdraw.",
        { h: "The third expedition" },
        "Moreira César left Monte Santo on 22 February with 1,281 men. The assault, in early March (the sources give the 3rd or 4th), went wrong because the attackers underestimated the enemy, the front of attack was poorly planned and the narrow alleys trapped the troops. The soldiers scattered through the lanes, facing guerrilla fighting. Moreira César was shot in the belly and back, leading a charge, and died before dawn. Colonel Tamarindo took over and made no firm decision. The retreat turned into a rout, the rear guard was cut off and destroyed, and Tamarindo died at the Angico stream. The backlanders kept the four cannon and a great deal of ammunition.",
        { h: "The fourth expedition" },
        "The response was of another order. Two columns, under Artur Oscar and General Cláudio do Amaral Savaget, with about 4,300 men, set out in mid-June and joined up on the Morro da Favela on 27 June, after combats at Cocorobó and Macambira. On the first day of fighting on the hill alone, the first column lost 524 men. Among the guns, the force had a Whitworth 32, nicknamed 'Matadeira'.",
        "On 18 July, about 3,350 men attacked in five brigades, and reached the eastern edge of the settlement, but stopped. The expedition lost nearly 1,000 men, dead and wounded, including three brigade commanders. They asked for 5,000 more soldiers. The government mobilized more than 2,900 men and Minister Bittencourt organized regular supply convoys.",
        "On 24 September, the encirclement was closed. The main attack, on 1 October, with 5,871 men, cost the Army 567 casualties and achieved little. On 2 October, about 300 women, children and elderly people surrendered. On 5 October, according to the most repeated account, the last four defenders died: two armed men, an old man and a child. The next day, the settlement was razed, with about 5,200 houses counted."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The numbers are the most debated of this war. Euclides da Cunha gave about 30,000 dead, of whom 25,000 were residents and 5,000 soldiers. The historian Robert Levine estimates 15,000, and another author, Joel Singer, comes to 5,000. Artur Oscar's commission counted 5,200 houses and 25,000 inhabitants, but other observers counted about 1,000 to 1,200 houses and fewer than 8,000 people.",
        "According to Portuguese Wikipedia, prisoners are estimated at between one and three thousand, and few men survived. Many had their throats cut, a practice called [[red necktie|gravata vermelha]], after, it is said, the minister told the general he had nowhere to keep them. Many died on the forced march to Alagoinhas, and some women are said to have been sent to brothels in Salvador. A Patriotic Committee helped save 146 women and children. Conselheiro's head was taken to the Faculty of Medicine of Bahia.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Canudos became the symbol of the distance between the Brazil of the coast and that of the backlands. Euclides da Cunha went to the front as a reporter and, in 'Os Sertões', accused the soldiers and their superiors, among them Minister Bittencourt. The most remembered line of the book is 'Canudos did not surrender'. The work is also debated: Euclides's scientific racism and his claims about who the residents were have been contested by later research.",
        "According to Walnice Nogueira Galvão, one of the leading scholars of the subject, after Canudos there would be no more danger of a restoration of the monarchy: the sacrifice of the backlanders served to consolidate the Republic.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The size of the town: Artur Oscar's commission counted 5,200 houses and 25,000 inhabitants. Other observers counted far fewer: from 1,000 to 1,400 houses and fewer than 8,000 residents." },
        { note: "The historian Dawid Bartelt disputes Euclides's idea that the ranch was deserted before Conselheiro arrived." },
        { note: "Euclides left two days before the end of the war and saw neither the fall of the settlement nor the massacre of prisoners." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  omdurman: {
    war: "Reconquest of Sudan (1896–1899)",
    hook: "On a morning in September 1898, cannon and machine guns killed about 11,000 Sudanese attacking on foot, at a cost of fewer than 50 lives on the other side.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1882, Britain took control of Egypt, and Sudan, which was Egyptian, was caught in the middle. In 1881, a religious leader named Muhammad Ahmad declared himself the [[Mahdi|mahdi]], the expected guide, and began a revolt against Egyptian rule. His followers, whom the Europeans called [[dervishes|dervixe]], destroyed an Egyptian army commanded by William Hicks at Shaykan, in November 1883. On 26 January 1885, they took Khartoum and killed the British general Charles Gordon and the whole garrison.",
        "The Mahdi died that same year. His successor, the caliph Abdullahi, made Omdurman, next to Khartoum, the capital of a harsh regime. Thirteen years later, Britain decided to return, to protect the Suez Canal and, in the government's words, to end the slave trade. There was also a desire to avenge Gordon.",
        { h: "The road to Omdurman" },
        { timeline: [
          ["21 Sept. 1896", "Kitchener takes Dongola."],
          ["7 Aug. 1897", "Takes Abu Hamed. The railway across the desert advances alongside."],
          ["7 Apr. 1898", "Defeats the Mahdists at the Atbara."],
          ["1 Sept. 1898", "The British army camps at Egeiga, a few kilometers from Omdurman."],
          ["2 Sept. 1898", "Battle of Omdurman."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Herbert Kitchener" },
        "A major general, he commanded the Egyptian army with the title of [[sirdar]]. The reconquest advanced slowly, with a railway across the desert, and he took Dongola, Abu Hamed and the Atbara before reaching Omdurman. He also wanted to avenge Gordon's death.",
        { h: "The caliph Abdullahi" },
        "A Mahdist leader, he had governed Omdurman since 1885 and commanded an army of tens of thousands of men. According to Kitchener's own dispatch, the British learned that he was thinking of a night attack. Kitchener prepared and, as a trick, had villagers from Egeiga hint that he himself would attack at night. The Caliph did not move, and the British army slept in peace inside the [[zariba]].",
        { h: "Other names" },
        "Hector MacDonald, commander of an Egyptian and Sudanese brigade, was the one who withstood the most dangerous attack. On the Mahdist side, Osman Azrak, Abdullah al-Taashi, Ali wad Hilu and Osman Sheikh ed-Din commanded separate groups. And the young lieutenant Winston Churchill, who also wrote as a reporter for the 'Morning Post' and took part in the charge of the 21st Lancers."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Anglo-Egyptian army had about 25,800 men, among 8,200 British and 17,600 Egyptians and Sudanese, with 52 rapid-fire guns and a flotilla of 12 gunboats on the Nile. The Mahdists were, depending on the source, from 35,000 to more than 50,000, including about 3,000 horsemen. The count by groups that Wikipedia makes adds up to about 53,000.",
        { note: "The Mahdist total is an estimate: the sources range from 35,000 to a little over 50,000." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Kitchener set the army in an arc around the village of Egeiga, with its back to the Nile and the [[zariba]] in front, facing a wide plain with hills on both sides: those of Kerreri, to the northwest, and that of Surkab (also called Jebel Surgham), to the west. Omdurman was less than 10 kilometers away, according to Kitchener's dispatch.",
        { h: "The battle in six movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, early in the morning, the Mahdist columns set out. About 8,000 men under Osman Azrak came from a ridge in front, and behind them, from the northwest, another 8,000. The guns opened fire at about 2,750 meters. The frontal attack collapsed, with some 4,000 dead, and nobody got within 50 meters of the British trenches.",
        "Second, Broadwood's cavalry tried to draw off part of the Mahdist forces, and the slower Camel Corps was chased by a large force of horsemen of the Green Standard. The Nile gunboats dispersed the pursuit before it reached the infantry's flank.",
        "Third, Kitchener advanced on Omdurman in columns. The 21st Lancers, with 320 to 400 men (the sources differ), went ahead. The scouts had seen 700 to 1,000 warriors in a depression, a [[khor]] called Abu Sunt. In fact, there were more than 2,000, hidden. The charge lasted less than two minutes, and the ditch was wider and deeper than expected. One officer and 20 soldiers died, and 4 officers and 46 soldiers were wounded. Of the 320 horses the source cites, 119 were killed or wounded. Three men of the regiment received the [[Victoria Cross|cruz vitória]]. Churchill commanded a troop of 25 lancers and fired his Mauser pistol at point-blank range.",
        "Fourth, the danger was at the other end. Kitchener turned left, going up the Surkab ridge and then south. MacDonald's brigade, of about 3,000 men, mostly Sudanese, came behind as rear guard, about 1,350 meters back, reinforced with Maxims and guns. The wounded and the supplies stayed at Egeiga, lightly guarded. From behind Surkab came about 15,000 Mahdists, who attacked MacDonald from the west, in two prongs. Lewis's Egyptian brigade held. MacDonald reorganized his battalions several times, and Kitchener sent other brigades to his rescue.",
        "Fifth, the end. The Mahdists broke. The northern force, Ali wad Hilu's, arrived too late and was repelled by Wauchope's brigade, which included the Lincolnshire Regiment. A last charge of about 500 horsemen was destroyed. At around 11:30, the march on Omdurman resumed.",
        "Sixth, the city. With the Mahdist army undone, the march ended in Omdurman. The Caliph escaped."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The Anglo-Egyptian army had 47 or 48 dead and 382 wounded, almost all under MacDonald's command and in the lancers' charge. On the Mahdist side, about 12,000 dead, 13,000 wounded and 5,000 prisoners, according to English Wikipedia. Older sources speak of about 11,000 dead.",
        "The Caliph escaped and died in 1899, at the battle of Umm Diwaykarat. After that, Anglo-Egyptian Sudan was born. Days after Omdurman, Kitchener was sent to Fashoda, where he met a French expedition, and the affair nearly led the two countries to war. He was made Baron Kitchener of Khartoum.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Omdurman became the symbol of the gap between industrialized armies and traditional armies in the age of imperialism. A line by a poet of the time, Hilaire Belloc, summed it up: 'Whatever happens, we have got the Maxim gun, and they have not.' It was also one of the last British cavalry charges, and the first major battle in which the Mark IV hollow-point bullet was used, according to English Wikipedia.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The killing of the wounded: after the battle, there were accusations that soldiers killed wounded Mahdists. The journalist Ernest Bennett attacked Kitchener in the 'Contemporary Review', and Bennet Burleigh defended him. Churchill, in private, agreed with Bennett, but softened the criticism in the 1902 edition of 'The River War'." },
        { note: "The Mahdi's tomb: the gunboats bombarded the dome of the tomb, and Kitchener ordered it destroyed so that it would not become a gathering point. The body was dug up and beheaded. A rumor circulated that he had kept the skull. The matter reached the British Parliament in 1899. The accounts vary on what happened to the remains." },
        { note: "The Victoria Crosses: the charge earned three for the 21st Lancers (Thomas Byrne, Raymond de Montmorency and Paul Kenna), all for saving comrades. A fourth, to Captain Nevill Smyth, of the 2nd Dragoon Guards, in the battle of the same day, was awarded to an officer of another regiment. In all, the battle had four." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  yalu1894: {
    war: "First Sino-Japanese War (1894–1895)",
    hook: "On an afternoon in the Yellow Sea, fast, well-trained Japanese ships defeated the great Chinese battleships, and China lost Korea.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "For centuries, Korea was a vassal of China. At the end of the 19th century, Japan, which had modernized quickly after 1868, wanted Korea for itself: as a market, as a defense and as a springboard to the continent. In 1894, a peasant revolt in Korea led both countries to send troops, and the war began.",
        "For Japan, sending soldiers and provisions to Korea depended on the sea. Whoever controlled the Yellow Sea would control the war. Both fleets were modern and bought, in large part, in Europe: the two largest ships of the Chinese one, the Beiyang Fleet, were German-built battleships.",
        { h: "The road to the Yalu" },
        { timeline: [
          ["25 July 1894", "At Pungdo, Japanese cruisers sink or capture ships escorting Chinese troops."],
          ["15 Sept. 1894", "The Japanese army wins at Pyongyang, in Korea."],
          ["16 Sept. 1894", "The Beiyang Fleet escorts five transports with about 4,500 soldiers from Dalian to the mouth of the Yalu."],
          ["17 Sept. 1894", "The two fleets meet in the battle of the Yalu River."]
        ]},
        "On the morning of the 17th, the Chinese fleet was anchored in shallow water, southwest of the river mouth, after leaving the soldiers ashore. Li Hongzhang, the viceroy who looked after the fleet, wanted to put off combat until the ammunition improved, but the court rejected it."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Admiral Itō Sukeyuki" },
        "He commanded the Japanese Combined Fleet aboard the cruiser Matsushima. He divided his ships into two groups: the Flying Squadron, of four fast cruisers, and the rest of the fleet. His advantage lay in speed and in [[rapid-fire guns|canhão de tiro rápido]], which the Japanese had in greater number.",
        { h: "Admiral Ding Ruchang" },
        "Head of the Beiyang Fleet, he relied on foreign advisers, among them the Prussian major Constantin von Hanneken and, aboard the battleship Zhenyuan, the American Philo McGiffin. Ding sailed in the Dingyuan, one of the two great battleships.",
        { h: "The captains" },
        "On the Japanese side, three names appear often later: Tōgō Heihachirō, commander of the cruiser Naniwa, and Kamimura Hikonojō, of the Akitsushima, who years later would be admirals in the war against Russia. On the Chinese side, Deng Shichang, captain of the Zhiyuan, died with his ship."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Japanese fleet had 12 ships: the Flying Squadron (Yoshino, Takachiho, Naniwa and Akitsushima, under Tsuboi Kōzō), the main group (the cruisers Matsushima, Chiyoda, Itsukushima and Hashidate and the old ironclads Fusō and Hiei), plus the gunboat Akagi and the armed steamer Saikyō Maru. The Chinese had 10 combat ships: the battleships Dingyuan and Zhenyuan, the cruisers Zhiyuan, Jingyuan, Laiyuan, Chaoyong, Yangwei and others. Later another group arrived, led by the Pingyuan.",
        "On paper, the Chinese had more heavily armored ships with more weight of fire. But most were old and poorly maintained, with little quality ammunition. The Japanese had many more rapid-fire guns.",
        { note: "The story of Chinese shells filled with cement or porcelain appears in several accounts, but the sources I consulted use the phrase 'according to reports'. McGiffin claimed that some of the powder was 13 years old and condemned. Corruption as the cause is the most repeated explanation, not a proven fact." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Open sea, southwest of the mouth of the Yalu River, with the Chinese fleet anchored in shallow water near the coast.",
        { h: "The battle in six movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, at 11:28, the Chinese lookouts saw smoke to the southwest. Ding tried to form a line abreast, but the signals got confused and the ships had different speeds, and they ended up in a wedge, with the two battleships in front. The Japanese came in column, with the Flying Squadron in the lead.",
        "Second, Itō sent the Flying Squadron against the Chinese right flank. The Chinese opened fire at about 5,000 meters, too far to hit. The Japanese held their fire for some twenty minutes, while crossing the enemy's front at high speed. Tsuboi went from 8 to 14 knots.",
        "Third, the Flying Squadron went around the Chinese right flank and fired only when at an effective distance. It battered the Chaoyong and the Yangwei, which caught fire and ran aground. Meanwhile, Itō's main group went around behind, and the Chinese fleet was caught between two fires.",
        "Fourth, the battleships Dingyuan and Zhenyuan withstood the punishment thanks to their armor, but the Japanese rapid-fire guns caused many casualties on deck. The Dingyuan's bridge was destroyed when the main guns fired too early, and the mast fell, cutting the flagship's signals.",
        "Fifth, the Jiyuan and the Guangjia turned and fled early. The Guangjia ran aground and was sunk by its own crew. On the Japanese side, the Matsushima was hit by two 12-inch shells that set off ready ammunition, and Itō shifted his flag to the Hashidate. The Hiei, slower, passed straight through the Chinese line and took several serious hits. The Akagi and the Saikyō Maru were damaged.",
        "Sixth, the Flying Squadron destroyed the Jingyuan and, in chasing the ship, let other Chinese ships escape into shallow water. At around 14:30 the Pingyuan arrived, with another ship and torpedo boats, which upset the plan to encircle the Chinese right wing. At 15:20, the burning Zhiyuan tried to ram the Naniwa (Chinese sources say it was the Yoshino) and sank, with Captain Deng Shichang. At sunset, the Chinese formation was near collapse, and the two battleships almost out of ammunition. The Japanese did not pursue them, in part because they did not know they were in that state."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "China lost five ships: Zhiyuan, Jingyuan, Chaoyong, Yangwei and Guangjia. Japan lost none, although four were seriously damaged. Chinese dead are given at about 850, with 500 wounded. The Japanese, about 180 dead and 200 wounded. The Matsushima had the heaviest casualties among the ships, more than a hundred dead and wounded.",
        "The remaining Chinese ships went to Lüshunkou and then to Weihaiwei, where the fleet was completely destroyed in the Japanese attack in early 1895. Beijing first denied the defeat and then blamed Li Hongzhang and Ding Ruchang, who were demoted, but still in post when the fleet was destroyed.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the most studied naval battle in the world after Lissa (1866). At Lissa, the victory came by ramming, with ships in line abreast; at the Yalu, by broadside fire, in [[line ahead|linha de fila]]. The lesson analysts drew was the value of rapid fire and crew training, not only of armor. By the Treaty of Shimonoseki, in 1895, China recognized Korea's independence and ceded Taiwan to Japan.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "'Almost a draw': some American analysts of the time, such as Secretary of the Navy Hilary Herbert, said the battle was almost a draw, and that better ammunition could have changed the result." },
        { note: "The Chinese command: the Chinese fleet's signal book was in English, which few officers read, according to English Wikipedia, and the ships followed the flagship's movements. There are divergent accounts of Ding's orders and of Von Hanneken's role." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

